Hydraulic head is the height water stands above a shared reference in a nonpumping well. Compare these two wells at the same moment.Left well is higherLeft well level is higherSelected setupBoth wells are levelBoth well levels matchCompare setupRight well is higherRight well level is higherCompare setupLeft well is higherLeft wellHigher headRight wellLower headBottom dashed line: the common reference.Short marks on each well: openings into the same aquifer.The well levels and water table are schematic. The grains show solid material with water in the connected spaces.Using these heads, which way would water flow horizontally?Toward the rightToward the leftNo net horizontal flowCheck flow directionMake a predictionChoose a direction, then check the well evidence.This is a simplified horizontal comparison in a uniform, connected aquifer. Water has the same density throughout, and neither well is pumping. Real groundwater can also move vertically. The drawing does not calculate flow rate, recharge, or changes caused by weather.

